    "## Task 6: CNN Concept Explanation\n\n### What is Convolution?\nConvolution is the fundamental operation in a CNN. A small weight matrix called a **filter** (typically 3\u00d73 or 5\u00d75) slides across the input image. At each position it computes a dot product between its weights and the corresponding image patch, producing a single number in the output **feature map**. Different filters learn to detect different patterns \u2014 one filter might detect horizontal edges, another diagonal lines, another colour blobs. By stacking many filters, a single convolutional layer extracts dozens of feature maps simultaneously. Crucially, the same filter weights are reused at every position (**weight sharing**), which drastically reduces parameters compared to a fully connected layer.\n\n### Why is Pooling Used?\nAfter convolution, the feature maps are still spatially large. **Max Pooling** divides each feature map into small non-overlapping windows (usually 2\u00d72) and keeps only the maximum value in each window. This:\n- **Reduces spatial size** (by half with a 2\u00d72 pool), cutting computation\n- **Provides translation invariance** \u2014 if a detected feature shifts slightly, the max in its window still fires\n- **Reduces overfitting** by removing fine-grained positional detail that might not generalise\n\n### Why is ReLU Commonly Used?\n`ReLU(x) = max(0, x)` turns all negative activations to zero and leaves positive ones unchanged. It is preferred over sigmoid/tanh for several reasons:\n- **Avoids vanishing gradients** \u2014 gradient is 1 for positive inputs, so learning does not stall in deep networks\n- **Computationally fast** \u2014 just a threshold comparison, much cheaper than exponential functions\n- **Sparse activations** \u2014 many neurons output zero, making the representation efficient and disentangled\n- **Empirically strong** \u2014 networks with ReLU train faster and often generalise better in practice\n\n### Why CNNs over Regular Feed-Forward Networks for Images?\n\n| | **CNN** | **Fully Connected MLP** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Weight sharing | \u2705 Same filter reused everywhere | \u274c Unique weight per pixel-neuron pair |\n| Parameters (64\u00d764 RGB input) | ~900 per 3\u00d73 filter | ~12.3 M per 1000-neuron layer |\n| Local pattern detection | \u2705 Naturally captures edges, textures | \u274c No spatial awareness |\n| Translation invariance | \u2705 Via convolution + pooling | \u274c Sensitive to exact pixel position |\n| Scalability | \u2705 Parameters grow with filter count, not image size | \u274c Explodes with image resolution |\n| Hierarchical features | \u2705 Edges \u2192 shapes \u2192 objects | \u274c Flat, unstructured |\n\nIn short, CNNs **exploit the spatial structure** of images by treating nearby pixels as related \u2014 which they are. An MLP has no such inductive bias and would need orders of magnitude more data and parameters to learn the same patterns.\n"
