    "## Task 7: Business Use Case Mapping\n\n### Domain: Manufacturing \u2014 Automated Visual Quality Control\n\n**Problem:**  \nTraditional manual visual inspection of manufactured parts (metal stampings, injection-moulded plastics, painted surfaces) is slow (one inspector per line), inconsistent (fatigue, subjectivity), and expensive. A single escaped defect can cause costly downstream failures or product recalls.\n\n**Solution \u2014 CNN-based Inline Inspection:**\n\n1. A line-scan or area-scan camera captures images of each part as it passes on the conveyor belt\n2. The image is segmented into patches and each patch is classified by the CNN in real time (<10 ms per part)\n3. Parts labelled as `dent`, `scratch`, or `stain` are automatically ejected to a reject bin\n4. All inspection results are logged with timestamps and images for traceability and root-cause analysis\n5. The model is periodically retrained on new production data to handle tool-wear, material changes, or new defect types\n\n**Tangible Benefits:**\n\n| Metric | Manual Inspection | CNN Inspection |\n|---|---|---|\n| Speed | ~60 parts / min | >2,000 parts / min |\n| Consistency | Variable (fatigue) | Constant 24/7 |\n| Defect escape rate | 2\u20135% | <0.5% |\n| Data for root cause | None | Full image log |\n| Cost per 1M parts inspected | High (labour) | Low (amortised HW) |\n\n**Real-World Deployments:**  \n- BMW and Audi use CNN inspection for body panel surface quality\n- Foxconn uses it for smartphone screen and housing defect detection  \n- Bosch uses similar systems for injection-moulded automotive components\n\nThis project's four-class model (dent / normal / scratch / stain) directly mirrors the defect taxonomy used in many ISO 10110 and ISO 8785 surface quality standards.\n"
